# Airflow ETL Pipeline: Transaction Fraud Detection

An automated ETL pipeline that ingests financial transaction data, cleans and transforms it, flags suspicious transactions, loads everything into PostgreSQL, and visualizes results in a Streamlit dashboard. Orchestrated with Apache Airflow and fully containerized with Docker.

## Pipeline Architecture

```
Raw CSV → Ingest → Clean → Transform → Fraud Flags → PostgreSQL → Streamlit Dashboard
                  (Apache Airflow DAGs orchestrate every step)
```

## Dashboard

![Dashboard Screenshot](docs/dashboard.png)

> Suspicious vs normal transactions at a glance.

## Tech Stack

| Area | Tools |
|---|---|
| Orchestration | Apache Airflow |
| Language / Processing | Python, Pandas |
| Database | PostgreSQL |
| Visualization | Streamlit |
| Containerization | Docker, Docker Compose |

## Features

* Automated ETL workflow built with Airflow DAGs (ingest, clean, transform, load)
* Data cleaning and transformation using Python and Pandas
* Rule-based fraud detection that flags suspicious transactions
* Summary analytics reports generated after each run
* Streamlit dashboard comparing suspicious vs normal transactions
* One-command setup with Docker Compose

## Fraud Detection Logic

Transactions are flagged as suspicious when they match rules such as:

* Amount above **[X]** threshold
* Unusually high transaction frequency in a short window
* **[add your other rule here]**

## Project Structure

```
airflow-etl-pipeline
│
├── ai-de-pipeline      # Core ETL pipeline logic
├── dags                # Airflow DAG workflows
├── data                # Transaction dataset
├── docker-compose.yaml # Docker configuration for Airflow services
└── .gitignore
```

## Getting Started

### Prerequisites

* Docker and Docker Compose installed

### Run it

```bash
git clone https://github.com/lohitha-25/airflow-etl-pipeline.git
cd airflow-etl-pipeline
docker compose up -d
```

(Older Docker versions: use `docker-compose up -d`.)

### Access

* **Airflow UI:** http://localhost:8080
  Login: `airflow` / `airflow` *(update if your setup uses different credentials)*
* **Streamlit Dashboard:** http://localhost:8501 *(update the port if different)*

Then enable the DAG in the Airflow UI and trigger a run.

